<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiberius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a> – 37 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiberius was the second Roman emperor, ruling from 14 to 37 CE as the stepson and adopted heir of Augustus. Before his accession he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s leading general, campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, Illyricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Great Illyrian Revolt</a> of 6–9 CE. As emperor he annexed Cappadocia and Commagene, and in his later years he withdrew to Capri, leaving Rome to the prefect Sejanus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiberius.jpg" alt="Tiberius" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Tiberius_Portrait_of_the_%27Imperium_Maius%27_type_in_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Augustus' conquest of the north required perhaps seven legions, and Strabo, writing under Augustus and <b>Tiberius</b>, describes three legions stationed in the province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained a free city under Augustus and a favoured retreat of Romans such as the future emperor <b>Tiberius</b>, who lived there from 6 BCE to 2 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Senators were forbidden to visit Egypt without the emperor's permission, and <b>Tiberius</b> rebuked his nephew Germanicus for touring the country in 19 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Roman rule, Augustus and <b>Tiberius</b> suppressed the druidic hierarchies and replaced them with Roman-style priesthoods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome suppressed the druids under <b>Tiberius</b> and Claudius, and Tacitus describes the Roman attack in 60 or 61 CE on their stronghold of Anglesey, where black-robed women with torches stood among the defenders.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic collapsed in the latter part of the 1st century BCE into what was a monarchy in form, if not in name; Tacitus judged the change irreversible only once <b>Tiberius</b> took power after the death of Augustus in 14 CE.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
